   * which are two to four characters and still one byte, and longer than
   * that in hex. The notation cannot walk the text, so each is worked
   * down to one character by substitution, a digit at a time, with
   * markers the lexer guarantees are not in a literal, control characters
   * below 32 that are not the tab already standing for `\\`.
   *
   * Hex first: `\x` becomes 01, 01 and a hex digit 02, 02 and one 03, and
   * 03 and one 0c, so 0c is a third digit, which is refused by name; C
   * reads every digit, and allows more than two only as zeros before the
   * last two. Then octal: `\` and a digit becomes 04, or 07 if the digit
   * is 4 to 7, and each takes up to two digits more, 05 and 06, 08 and 0b.
   * 0b is three digits led by a 4 or more, past 255, refused. After that,
   * every escape is one character or begins with the one backslash it has
   * left, and the old count holds. `snum` says whether there was a numeric
   * escape at all, by whether a backslash went, since only those strings
   * can hold a NUL. `numeric-escapes-every-digit.c` writes each digit in
   * each place the substitutions look for one. *)
